Your current maven-dependency-plugin creates a target/lib directory; it does not merge everything into one JAR.

For a single fat/uber JAR containing your project + dependencies, use maven-shade-plugin. Apache's current documentation shows 3.6.2 and the ManifestResourceTransformer for executable JARs. 

Replace the maven-dependency-plugin block with this:

<plugin>
    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
    <artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
    <version>3.6.2</version>

    <executions>
        <execution>
            <phase>package</phase>

            <goals>
                <goal>shade</goal>
            </goals>

            <configuration>

                <!-- Keep original jar and create a second fat jar -->
                <shadedArtifactAttached>true</shadedArtifactAttached>

                <!-- Produces: ImpalaStressTesting-1.0-SNAPSHOT-jar-all.jar -->
                <shadedClassifierName>jar-all</shadedClassifierName>

                <!-- Don't generate dependency-reduced-pom.xml -->
                <createDependencyReducedPom>false</createDependencyReducedPom>

                <!--
                    Signed dependency JARs can cause:
                    "Invalid signature file digest"
                    after being merged into one JAR.
                -->
                <filters>
                    <filter>
                        <artifact>*:*</artifact>
                        <excludes>
                            <exclude>META-INF/*.SF</exclude>
                            <exclude>META-INF/*.DSA</exclude>
                            <exclude>META-INF/*.RSA</exclude>
                        </excludes>
                    </filter>
                </filters>

                <transformers>

                    <!--
                        Merge META-INF/services entries from dependencies.
                        Important for JDBC and libraries using ServiceLoader.
                    -->
                    <transformer implementation=
                        "org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.ServicesResourceTransformer"/>

                    <!-- Make the fat JAR executable -->
                    <transformer implementation=
                        "org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.ManifestResourceTransformer">

                        <mainClass>
                            com.cib.impalaStressTester.ImpalaStressTester
                        </mainClass>

                    </transformer>

                </transformers>

            </configuration>
        </execution>
    </executions>
</plugin>

So your <build> section should look approximately like:

<build>

    <plugins>

        <plugin>
            <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
            <version>3.15.0</version>

            <configuration>
                <source>11</source>
                <target>11</target>
            </configuration>
        </plugin>


        <plugin>
            <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
            <version>3.6.2</version>

            <executions>
                <execution>

                    <phase>package</phase>

                    <goals>
                        <goal>shade</goal>
                    </goals>

                    <configuration>

                        <shadedArtifactAttached>true</shadedArtifactAttached>

                        <shadedClassifierName>
                            jar-all
                        </shadedClassifierName>

                        <createDependencyReducedPom>
                            false
                        </createDependencyReducedPom>

                        <filters>

                            <filter>

                                <artifact>*:*</artifact>

                                <excludes>
                                    <exclude>META-INF/*.SF</exclude>
                                    <exclude>META-INF/*.DSA</exclude>
                                    <exclude>META-INF/*.RSA</exclude>
                                </excludes>

                            </filter>

                        </filters>

                        <transformers>

                            <transformer implementation=
                                "org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.ServicesResourceTransformer"/>

                            <transformer implementation=
                                "org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.ManifestResourceTransformer">

                                <mainClass>
                                    com.cib.impalaStressTester.ImpalaStressTester
                                </mainClass>

                            </transformer>

                        </transformers>

                    </configuration>

                </execution>
            </executions>

        </plugin>

    </plugins>

</build>

The service-resource transformer is useful when dependencies use META-INF/services; Shade supports transformers specifically for combining resources when dependencies are merged. 

Then build it:

mvn clean package

You should get two JARs under:

target\

Something like:

ImpalaStressTesting-1.0-SNAPSHOT.jar
ImpalaStressTesting-1.0-SNAPSHOT-jar-all.jar

The second one is the one you want:

ImpalaStressTesting-1.0-SNAPSHOT-jar-all.jar

You can check its size:

dir target\*.jar

And verify that Hikari is inside it:

jar tf target\ImpalaStressTesting-1.0-SNAPSHOT-jar-all.jar | findstr "HikariDataSource"

You should find:

com/zaxxer/hikari/HikariDataSource.class

Verify the Impala JDBC driver too:

jar tf target\ImpalaStressTesting-1.0-SNAPSHOT-jar-all.jar | findstr "impala/jdbc41/Driver"

You want:

com/cloudera/impala/jdbc41/Driver.class

Then because we set your main class in MANIFEST.MF, you no longer need:

mvn exec:java -Dexec.mainClass="com.cib.impalaStressTester.ImpalaStressTester"

You can run the generated fat JAR directly:

java -jar target\ImpalaStressTesting-1.0-SNAPSHOT-jar-all.jar

One thing I'd specifically verify for this project is the Cloudera driver, because you've been getting:

The POM for com.cloudera.impala.jdbc:ImpalaJDBC41:jar:2.6.4.1005 is invalid

That warning means Maven cannot discover that driver's transitive dependencies from its POM. The fat JAR can only include dependencies Maven actually knows about. So after building, checking that Driver.class is present is important. If runtime then reports a missing Cloudera class, we'll need to package the full JDBC41 driver distribution dependencies into the fat JAR as well.
